Z||000010||olliver||11-03-2001||12:00 PM||||You are not going to want to hear this.  But last summer when i had HUGE alt problems, i learned the quickest and easiest way to replace the alt on my '86 auto gt.  And here it is:<P>Step 1 - The four bolts that hold the alt together in the casing, undo them.<P>Step 2 - Before pulling the case apart, mark one of the sides (both halves) for a reference to put it back together.  Now pull apart the case.  <P>Step 3 - On the bottom half of the alt, where you see the brushes for the motor.  There is a hole in the case.  Get a paper clip straighten it out, push it thru the hole so that the brushes are held in their slot (you will see the channels where the brushes are in and behind them are springs.  You have to compress the spring to get the paper clip to hold the brushes in place.<P>Step 3 - Slide in both halves into the engine compartment where the alt is located.<P>Step 4 - Align both halves with the reference mark you made.<P>Step 5 - Slide both halves together <P>Step 6 - Replace 4 bolts and remove paper clip<P>Step 7 - Slide alt back into bracket, attach belt and connect connector and power wire, tighten to spec.<P>Note:<BR>     I know it sounds like a pain, but you should be able to do it in like 30-40 mins.<P>EXTREME NOTE:  Do not disconnect only the positive side of the battery, if you do you will fry the voltage regulator as soon as you connect it.  You must disconnect both terminals of the battery.<P>And that's how i did it.<P>Olliver||||reg||1
Z||000011||jeffndebrus||11-03-2001||12:53 PM||||oh my gosh!<BR>olliver did you really do that --- should I???  do I have to?  Oh my gosh!!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||olliver||11-03-2001||01:40 PM||||No word of a lie.  I had to do that like 4 times.  The one that i got from the store was faulty to begin with, then i cooked the regulator on the second one.  Finally the third one went in fine.||||reg||1
