Z||000015||MrFieroUK||11-03-2001||03:05 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> Please remember to replace the dogbone after refitting the alternator??!! It helps to remove the dogbone support bracket mounted to the engine also. It really doesn't take more than a few minutes and with the blower tube for the alternator/coil also out of the way, you should have plenty of room to get the alt. back in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000016||maryjane||11-03-2001||03:59 PM||||I had the same prob with my Jeep Cherokee. Alt virtually fell out once I got the bolts out, but couldn't get the new one back in the brackets for my life. Jane came out & did it in 30 seconds, but had a wire hung up behind it. I took it out, moved the wire and AGAIN couldn't get it in place. I had to get her to put it in. A little embarrassed.||||reg||1
Z||000017||jeffndebrus||11-03-2001||11:15 PM||||Thank you all for your help.  The bracket had to come out and then the alternator was lifted into its aproximate location.  then the bracket was slid up around the alternator and bolted back in place. then it was not hard to bolt in the alternator. <P> I just hope that the car doesn't start throwing belts again!!!That was the reason the previous owner parked it.||||reg||1
Z||000018||samoht||11-03-2001||11:16 PM||||are we talking about a V6.. I changed mine in like 10 minutes.. I push it up from the bottom ( through the craddle ) Wedge it above it and got to top, and put top bolt hrough...  I got wrong alternater twice and it was tight ..  DO NOT EVER CLOCK AN ALTERNATOR ..  Make sure your click it away from the motor<P>85gt3.2||||reg||1
